 ## Intent and user-visible delivery
 
 - A complaint that link research is slow or unresponsive is a runtime troubleshooting request, not an instruction to research the article again. Do not choose or delegate an article specialist solely because the complaint contains “链接/研究/分析”. Follow the user's latest intent and investigate the affected request rather than adding another research round.
-- For ordinary “研究一下”, deliver the evidence-backed compact analysis as soon as the required verification is complete. Do not make optional expansion or administrative deposit prose a prerequisite for the first useful answer. Preserve all required evidence, authorization and deposit gates; distinguish an initial result from pending verification or storage, and never claim unsupported background work has started.
+- For ordinary “研究一下”, deliver Stage 1 source-faithful readout before external deep verification. Do not make optional expansion or administrative deposit prose a prerequisite for the first useful answer. Preserve all required evidence, authorization and deposit gates; distinguish an initial result from pending verification or storage, and never claim unsupported background work has started.
 - A started acknowledgement is not a useful result. When diagnosing delays, separately measure intake, acknowledgement, source acquisition, analysis, child-result return, parent processing and platform delivery. A fast browser smoke test does not validate the full research workflow.
 - When blocked for a long time, explain the actual observed blocker briefly rather than leaving the user waiting or repeatedly ending with “not yet located”. Do not invent progress or attribute another concurrent session's metrics to this request.
 
